#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272063 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129213 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "434a82c2-7964-525a-85b9-eb17fa7b5b26" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Connections Between Matrices of a Linear Transformation Relative to Different Bases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09f81da2-1a75-5f55-8d79-8c2274041ce5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269358} {:answer/id #uuid "66275c26-ff38-5753-9c00-3c8ab5e52584" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270177} {:answer/id #uuid "ab9a1869-b9bb-5e1a-86a3-a0b65d7624de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270801} {:answer/id #uuid "b1aef7d0-44ca-54af-bea9-b756d13ca4e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270860} {:answer/id #uuid "e4c16bc5-32d2-569b-946e-08ac6935f2fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271286}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "66275c26-ff38-5753-9c00-3c8ab5e52584" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270177} :answer-field/id #uuid "3304b220-96b4-54f2-aaaf-0f382e67ec76" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271602}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cf5001e6-d49e-526a-bbd2-b37f328e04e1" :question/math-academy-id "q-140361" :question/problem "Let $[T]_{B}$ and $[T]_{C}$ be the matrices of a linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C,$ respectively. If ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from basis $B$ to basis $C,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $[T]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}$\n- $[T]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1}$\n- $[T]_{B}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{C}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1}$" :question/worked-solution "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](images/1b/1b81504d6fdcabee0e1f586fef3dd2af5a57a8411ba4d22429e00e09b171a4d1.png)\n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$\n\nSolving this equation for $[T]_{B},$ we obtain the alternative formulation\n\n$$\n[T]_{B}={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1}\\cdot [T]_{C}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272064 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129213 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "434a82c2-7964-525a-85b9-eb17fa7b5b26" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Connections Between Matrices of a Linear Transformation Relative to Different Bases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c42a1c1-86fe-59bc-9f0a-3e1bd222c784" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269502} {:answer/id #uuid "48c52bab-433b-55d3-b20b-830726dde6f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269934} {:answer/id #uuid "b96f8a18-200d-579a-a147-0023f2922dab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270906} {:answer/id #uuid "ce1de8a8-2fd8-5db0-9470-db2fec46edbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271077} {:answer/id #uuid "d8cc3afe-be4c-5e2f-83f2-6d76c7314bd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271172}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b96f8a18-200d-579a-a147-0023f2922dab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270906} :answer-field/id #uuid "29078bb2-cd84-54b6-9b00-8b8a30600335" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271586}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bc68e9b9-f221-584c-883c-3c892a06e266" :question/math-academy-id "q-140362" :question/problem "Let $[T]_{B}$ and $[T]_{C}$ be the matrices of a linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C,$ respectively. If ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from basis $B$ to basis $C,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $[T]_{B}={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1}\\cdot [T]_{C}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}$\n- $[T]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}$\n- $[T]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{C}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1}$" :question/worked-solution "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](images/1b/1b81504d6fdcabee0e1f586fef3dd2af5a57a8411ba4d22429e00e09b171a4d1.png)\n\n \n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$\n\nSolving this equation for $[T]_{B},$ we obtain the alternative formulation\n\n$$\n[T]_{B}={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1}\\cdot [T]_{C}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272092 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124571 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef311af0-0e95-5574-a151-2971ad66bd08" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Eigenvalues of a 4x4 Matrix Containing a Block of Zeros"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "025bf3dc-65a2-5980-9de5-504713e44ac7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269290} {:answer/id #uuid "6965124d-345b-50d1-85a1-e0688b3be84e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270203} {:answer/id #uuid "77bcfcf4-a25d-5a49-946d-144dcf244a05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270330} {:answer/id #uuid "85c3df0c-47d2-59a5-8213-c08edc670bff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270426} {:answer/id #uuid "a2695c9b-1195-5c04-ad47-6a93a769893e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270714}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "85c3df0c-47d2-59a5-8213-c08edc670bff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270426} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d987ec8-4c29-5fef-b7be-dd652491072d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271731}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a042e23b-9a4b-5f08-a06a-17d78f4dd358" :question/math-academy-id "q-140450" :question/problem "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 & -6 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 7 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -4 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $P$ given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\lambda =0$ is an eigenvalue of $P$\n- $\\lambda =-4$ is an eigenvalue of $P$\n- The eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ has algebraic multiplicity $2$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the matrix $\\,P-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nP-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}5-\\lambda  & 2 & -6 & 8 \\\\ 0 & -\\lambda  & 0 & 7 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -4-\\lambda  & 9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe characteristic equation is $det(P-\\lambda I)=0.$ \n\nNotice that we can expand the $4\\times 4$ determinant across the $1$st column, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(P-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}5-\\lambda  & 2 & -6 & 8 \\\\ 0 & -\\lambda  & 0 & 7 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -4-\\lambda  & 9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (5-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}-\\lambda  & 0 & 7 \\\\ -3 & -4-\\lambda  & 9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, expanding the $3\\times 3$ determinant across the $3$rd row, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(5-\\lambda )\\cdot (2-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ -3 & -4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (5-\\lambda )(2-\\lambda )(-\\lambda )(-4-\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -5)(\\lambda -2)\\lambda (\\lambda +4) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the eigenvalues of $P$ are $\\lambda =5,$ $\\lambda =2,$ $\\lambda =0,$ and $\\lambda =-4$  (all with algebraic multiplicity $1$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272117 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133791 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e81030a-0151-5dea-b9bc-74aae2499482" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Orthogonal Projection Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15d1ff54-798c-5495-ac77-7c70131ecd65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269455} {:answer/id #uuid "699d0687-452d-5bb8-8095-db807468d7c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270205} {:answer/id #uuid "8e661237-401b-59f7-9700-6fd117cb0fe8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270502} {:answer/id #uuid "c0e2da0a-9db2-5571-a16f-ea09cd190f8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270979} {:answer/id #uuid "eecf636f-9554-5ca1-92d5-73c878cfccc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271367}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c0e2da0a-9db2-5571-a16f-ea09cd190f8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270979} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff75cce5-5510-50fb-bc04-9b62533591ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271954}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6043910d-249c-5dcb-8998-243aef51387f" :question/math-academy-id "q-140516" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${A}^{T}=A$\n- ${A}^{2}=A$\n- $A$ is an orthogonal projection matrix" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $Q$ is an orthogonal projection matrix if and only if ${Q}^{2}=Q$ and ${Q}^{T}=Q.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false since ${A}^{T}\\ne A.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2} & =A\\cdot A \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false since not both statements I and II are true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \" II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272132 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133872 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "382a6db7-2708-5711-885e-6f3f3037e8df" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Set of Functions is Orthogonal With Respect to a Given Inner Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0562db51-66a7-5a18-a492-af715264f543" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269315} {:answer/id #uuid "71005342-d55f-5332-b578-95e6f906faab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270273} {:answer/id #uuid "a0c3fac8-64b6-560e-a9cd-252aed9b1291" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270694} {:answer/id #uuid "e3d033de-73ce-5f72-b75d-36907f8d890e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271275} {:answer/id #uuid "f223e07f-474f-53ea-9772-c00afbe6a869" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271394}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a0c3fac8-64b6-560e-a9cd-252aed9b1291" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270694} :answer-field/id #uuid "7165e98a-3520-5bd3-93ee-a5107bf92b82" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271710}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1e4b04d4-e7d3-5042-809a-2a3512a7a902" :question/math-academy-id "q-140556" :question/problem "Consider the following set of functions:\n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(t)=2(t-1),\\,{f}_{2}(t)={e}^{t},\\,{f}_{3}(t)={e}^{(t-1)^{2}}\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space $C[0,2]$ of continuous functions over $[0,2]$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{2}f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nII. ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n\nIII. $S=\\{{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{3}(t)\\}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is called orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩ & =⟨2(t-1),{e}^{t}⟩ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}2(t-1)({e}^{t})\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's use integration by parts. We denote\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=2(t-1) & ⟹ & {u}^{′}=2, \\\\ {v}^{′}={e}^{t} & ⟹ & v=\\int {v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}t=\\int {e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t={e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, applying the integration by parts formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}2(t-1){e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t & =uv{|}_{0}^{2}-{\\int }_{0}^{2}{u}^{′}v\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(2(t-1){e}^{t}){|}_{0}^{2}-{\\int }_{0}^{2}2{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(2{e}^{2}-(-2))-2{e}^{t}{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =2{e}^{2}+2-(2{e}^{2}-2) \\\\  & =4\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥̸{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨2(t-1),{e}^{(t-1)^{2}}⟩ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}2(t-1){e}^{(t-1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={e}^{(t-1)^{2}}{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =e-e \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${f}_{1}(t)⊥̸{f}_{3}(t),$ the set $S$ is *not* orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272159 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124528 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "20a6c0cf-04d4-5908-a03f-1adf491b9282" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem in Terms of Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09787070-5733-5c1f-945a-b8fade9da8b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269355} {:answer/id #uuid "4ba01b4f-8964-54bd-b8f1-feba4517a205" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269949} {:answer/id #uuid "94ab7e7b-5ee0-5fef-a0ba-ef59e46d7fb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270572} {:answer/id #uuid "9eb5dee7-98f6-5027-ad6e-e7a62c557037" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270674} {:answer/id #uuid "f420726b-eeff-586b-8e0a-647d2d964209" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271409}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "09787070-5733-5c1f-945a-b8fade9da8b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269355} :answer-field/id #uuid "8dfbd69f-3a9f-5022-b832-bc4aefd85166" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271761}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1812a9f8-6cf3-5d8f-b5c7-1adf1d688a91" :question/math-academy-id "q-140835" :question/problem "A matrix $A$ has the characteristic equation $2{\\lambda }^{3}-3{\\lambda }^{2}-4=0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\lambda =2$ is a root of the characteristic equation\n- $\\lambda =0$ is an eigenvalue of $A$\n- $A$ is invertible" :question/worked-solution "The invertible matrix theorem in terms of eigenvectors states the following:\n\n*The square matrix $A$ is invertible if and only if $0$ is not an eigenvalue of $A.$*\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, substituting $\\lambda =2$ into the equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\lambda }^{3}-3{\\lambda }^{2}-4 & =0 \\\\ 2(2)^{3}-3(2)^{2}-4 & =0 \\\\ 16-12-4 & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $\\lambda =0$ is not a root of the characteristic equation, $\\lambda =0$ is not an eigenvalue of $A.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $\\lambda =0$ is not an eigenvalue of $A.$ As a result, according to the invertible matrix theorem, $A$ is invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272160 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124605 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c34e7d59-6bb4-5c2f-808f-212503d55338" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02780196-fa75-5062-a6b7-0cc51285054c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269291} {:answer/id #uuid "06658ca9-e22b-55a3-8fe2-e2d7a44c4438" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269326} {:answer/id #uuid "8f2e262b-113e-5bd8-aeda-f355760ebb0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270516} {:answer/id #uuid "9616fe83-1849-50bd-9922-51207d18a239" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270588} {:answer/id #uuid "c5def0e8-1b57-557b-b93f-16d8d092b9bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271014}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "02780196-fa75-5062-a6b7-0cc51285054c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269291} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e003ad9-af8d-5f1c-83de-8cc274501c50" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271677}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "47d1848e-d69a-5675-a8fe-fca5e30be264" :question/math-academy-id "q-140838" :question/problem "If $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $\\lambda =-4$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $(A+4I)x=0$ has a nonzero solution\n- $\\text{Col}(A+4I)={R}^{2}$\n- $\\text{nullity}(A+4I)=0$" :question/worked-solution "Given a $2\\times 2$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{2} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=2=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nLet's now apply the theorem to the matrix $M=A+4I.$\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that if $\\lambda =4$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ then the nonzero solutions of $(A+4I)x=0$ are eigenvectors of $A.$ As a result, the matrix $M=A+4I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statements II and III are false. Since $M=A+4I$ is singular, by the invertible matrix theorem, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\text{Col}(A+4I)\\ne {R}^{2},\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A+4I)\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272161 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124529 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "30d1a3a3-a6f0-522c-9161-6dc0fefb2e0d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix and Rank-Nullity Theorems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "75c45048-6da9-5e66-9999-d55d942f1986" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270309} {:answer/id #uuid "8be0f024-03a9-5fcb-8b70-e4bd7fa12b77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270481} {:answer/id #uuid "9c33154b-55e0-5408-a9c3-b312471c1b83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270647} {:answer/id #uuid "aab925ad-4045-522f-8476-b618a445ccbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270795} {:answer/id #uuid "e4736cff-c2ee-574d-b818-12f2036f2384" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271282}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e4736cff-c2ee-574d-b818-12f2036f2384" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271282} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4e537fe-7191-5ea1-8bce-61de982dbea3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271933}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f77ae0b2-0cbc-5c99-a8dc-82a79dae8fdc" :question/math-academy-id "q-140840" :question/problem "If $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $\\text{rank}(A-7I)=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A-7I$ is singular\n- $\\text{nullity}(A-7I)=0$\n- $\\lambda =7$ is an eigenvalue of $A$" :question/worked-solution "Given a $2\\times 2$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{2} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=2=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $M$ has $2$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(M)+\\text{nullity}(M)=2.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statements I is true. By the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{nullity}(A-7I)\\ne 0,$ we obtain that $A-7I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(A-7I) & =2-\\text{rank}(A-7I) \\\\  & =2-0 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Notice that if $M=A-7I$ is singular, then $(A-7I)x=0$ has nonzero solutions which will be eigenvectors of $A.$ As a result, $\\lambda =7$ is an eigenvalue of $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272162 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124529 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "30d1a3a3-a6f0-522c-9161-6dc0fefb2e0d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix and Rank-Nullity Theorems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37c3ab4a-d011-567e-a4bb-8e9d36570202" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269773} {:answer/id #uuid "486195e9-68e7-52bc-a0d1-cda7e04826cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269928} {:answer/id #uuid "7623ec57-6450-5b90-acba-bcedc4817b39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270312} {:answer/id #uuid "9df4d4f0-b93f-5520-888d-09db2b51a7f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270668} {:answer/id #uuid "b9dd595d-5db6-5f4c-8302-dae9d8ff3b2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270911}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7623ec57-6450-5b90-acba-bcedc4817b39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270312} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c037997-12d3-5b7c-aa0f-1a879bb19a43" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271704}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "78a8d2b4-93c9-5ecc-ada3-6436c09c3461" :question/math-academy-id "q-140842" :question/problem "If $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $\\text{nullity}(A+8I)=2,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A+8I$ is singular\n- $\\lambda =-8$ is an eigenvalue of $A$\n- $\\text{rank}(A+8I)=2$" :question/worked-solution "Given a $3\\times 3$ matrix $M,$ we have the following by the invertible matrix theorem:\n\n$M$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(M)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(M))=3=\\text{rank}(M) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(M)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(M))=0=\\text{nullity}(M)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $M$ has $3$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(M)+\\text{nullity}(M)=3.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statements I is true. By the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{nullity}(A+8I)\\ne 0,$ we obtain that $A+8I$ must be singular.\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice that if $M=A+8I$ is singular, then $(A+8I)x=0$ has nonzero solutions which will be eigenvectors of $A.$ As a result, $\\lambda =-8$ is an eigenvalue of $A.$\n\n- Statement III is false. According to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A+8I) & =3-\\text{nullity}(A+8I) \\\\  & =3-2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272182 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184220 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc36f534-6b27-5619-a29e-9954d0310adb" :knowledge-point/title "Verifying Continuity of Two-Piece Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11cec077-c70f-523b-8da0-293ef503e9bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269426} {:answer/id #uuid "4acd5f49-665e-5cd3-8d90-5a84ccec631f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269944} {:answer/id #uuid "aeb28856-23da-5eab-9761-5cc5a1fa6401" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270822} {:answer/id #uuid "dc6e7269-21de-58a6-9e79-d467bce4e42d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271199} {:answer/id #uuid "e9d79f8c-1051-5516-9d4e-f14a14271dc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271318}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4acd5f49-665e-5cd3-8d90-5a84ccec631f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269944} :answer-field/id #uuid "c57c2e77-1d97-5e77-a7c0-cee20a1d0d4d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271868}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f3fd6961-1be3-5be2-829e-70725d6ca86c" :question/math-academy-id "q-141001" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f(x),$ defined below?\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2},\\, & x\\le 1 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x),\\, & x>1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $f(x)$ is left-continuous at $x=1$\n- $f(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=1$\n- $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=1$" :question/worked-solution "We're required to determine the left and right continuity properties of $f(x)$ at $x=1.$\n\nLet's start by computing $f(1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1) & =-{1}^{2} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. The function is left-continuous at $x=1$ since the left-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & =-{1}^{2} \\\\  & =-1 \\\\  & =f(1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. The function is right-continuous at $x=1$ since the right-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 1) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =-1 \\\\  & =f(1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. The function is both left and right-continuous at $x=1,$ which implies that the function is continuous at $x=1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272183 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184220 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc36f534-6b27-5619-a29e-9954d0310adb" :knowledge-point/title "Verifying Continuity of Two-Piece Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5bb9afd2-a23b-5213-8b6f-0774be368ae6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270084} {:answer/id #uuid "6dcf50eb-9b14-5609-9e07-45ac00f5a323" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270246} {:answer/id #uuid "85788f2a-421f-595a-a301-4a9f34043c84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270423} {:answer/id #uuid "9a42944f-c4d0-5902-b7e6-420fbb0680cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270626} {:answer/id #uuid "de31b10c-345d-5d28-af4f-62601a087cbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271218}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "85788f2a-421f-595a-a301-4a9f34043c84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270423} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe3cb052-ae4e-5b7b-89f7-2a9670a41e56" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271951}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ce7bb2c8-a5d2-5cd9-93b3-f572c13f436f" :question/math-academy-id "q-141002" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $h(x),$ defined below?\n\n$$\nh(x)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-27,\\, & x<3 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2),\\, & x\\ge 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $h(x)$ is left-continuous at $x=3$\n- $h(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=3$\n- $h(x)$ is continuous at $x=3$" :question/worked-solution "We're required to determine the left and right continuity properties of $h(x)$ at $x=3.$\n\nLet's start by computing $h(3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(3) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3-2) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡1 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. The function is left-continuous at $x=3$ since the left-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}h(x) & ={3}^{3}-27 \\\\  & =27-27 \\\\  & =0 \\\\  & =h(3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. The function is right-continuous at $x=3$ since the right-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}h(x) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3-2) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡1 \\\\  & =0 \\\\  & =h(3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. The function is both left and right-continuous at $x=3,$ which implies that the function is continuous at $x=3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272226 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133766 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "42258cf7-c1cf-5cc3-876d-102cc2233083" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Hermitian Inner Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0aad624f-4b38-5923-afbd-1579952dbf84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269364} {:answer/id #uuid "3b956d8b-d0de-5dcf-817b-4188dc3372de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269811} {:answer/id #uuid "591adc53-138b-519d-9fb5-74c10f838635" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270055} {:answer/id #uuid "f0f1c832-b011-546e-af5d-c70d12222e25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271386} {:answer/id #uuid "fdaa9cbc-8e1e-5488-8e27-7caa05c408ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271489}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3b956d8b-d0de-5dcf-817b-4188dc3372de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269811} :answer-field/id #uuid "85a148f5-fd30-5518-8877-e3dc571076f9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271743}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "525bde81-7550-5f13-8e28-5c16a04ee9f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-141159" :question/problem "Consider the standard Hermitian inner product $⟨\\,\\cdot \\,,\\,\\cdot \\,⟩$ over $C.$ \nWhich of the following statements are true? \n\n- $⟨a,b⟩=⟨b,a⟩$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$\n- $⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in R$\n- $⟨a,a⟩>0$ for any $a\\in {C}^{n}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the standard Hermitian inner product over $C$ satisfies the following properties:\n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a+b,c⟩=⟨a,c⟩+⟨b,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨\\alpha a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b+c⟩=⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\overset{―}{\\alpha }⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $a,b\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number, and $⟨a,a⟩=0$ if and only if $a=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The inner product over $C$ has conjugate symmetry.\n\n- Statement II is true. For any $\\alpha \\in R,$ we have $\\overset{―}{\\alpha }=\\alpha .$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\overset{―}{\\alpha }⟨a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Notice that if $a=0,$ then $⟨a,a⟩=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272227 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133766 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "42258cf7-c1cf-5cc3-876d-102cc2233083" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Hermitian Inner Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e483cc2-b46f-5512-a9a7-0f10ea8a953d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269394} {:answer/id #uuid "4288a7d7-b334-57bd-981d-9101054485a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269869} {:answer/id #uuid "4bf5a7d9-2cd0-5edb-8159-5892ad0bc218" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269954} {:answer/id #uuid "6d857f4b-9335-543f-9a26-45ba2569561b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270242} {:answer/id #uuid "cf364781-4d57-5ea3-a715-fb67ec1553ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271085}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6d857f4b-9335-543f-9a26-45ba2569561b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270242} :answer-field/id #uuid "672798eb-6f95-52b3-882f-b5bf772f3083" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271694}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "396a82c3-ba69-5d62-8d39-94fffd303517" :question/math-academy-id "q-141160" :question/problem "Consider the standard Hermitian inner product $⟨\\,\\cdot \\,,\\,\\cdot \\,⟩$ over $C.$ \nWhich of the following statements are true? \n\n- $⟨ia,b⟩=i⟨a,b⟩$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$\n- $⟨a,\\overset{―}{\\alpha }b⟩=⟨\\alpha a,b⟩$ for any $a,b\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C$\n- $⟨ia,a⟩\\in R$ for any $a\\in {C}^{n}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the standard Hermitian inner product over $C$ satisfies the following properties:\n\n- It is linear in the first argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a+b,c⟩=⟨a,c⟩+⟨b,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨\\alpha a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It is antilinear in the second argument. For any $a,b,c\\in {C}^{n}$ and any $\\alpha \\in C,$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b+c⟩=⟨a,b⟩+⟨a,c⟩\\,$ and $\\,⟨a,\\alpha b⟩=\\overset{―}{\\alpha }⟨a,b⟩.$\n\n- It has conjugate symmetry. For any $a,b\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,b⟩=\\overset{―}{⟨b,a⟩}.$\n\n- It is positive-definite. For any $a\\in {C}^{n},$ we have\n\n$\\,$ $⟨a,a⟩$ is a non-negative real number, and $⟨a,a⟩=0$ if and only if $a=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is true.  Indeed, the inner product in ${C}^{n}$ is linear in the first argument. So, a constant multiple from the first argument can be factored out.\n\n- Statement II is true. By the antilinearity of a complex inner product in the second argument and linearity in the first argument, we have \n\n$$\n⟨a,\\overset{―}{\\alpha }b⟩=\\overset{―}{(\\overset{―}{\\alpha })}⟨a,b⟩=\\alpha ⟨a,b⟩=⟨\\alpha a,b⟩.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Notice that $⟨a,a⟩\\in R$ for any $a\\in {C}^{n}.$ Therefore, if $a\\ne 0,$ then\n\n$$\n⟨ia,a⟩=i⟨a,a⟩\\notin R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272238 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124511 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "084a0a83-5ce6-5184-beb7-8ae7b4f1a799" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "74ef5d4c-f33d-597d-a2fa-aa2bd980b69e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270302} {:answer/id #uuid "91213c6e-a951-5f10-ad74-2238bac4f2f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270532} {:answer/id #uuid "945a196e-773a-511b-a5a8-a31f4dcb23f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270565} {:answer/id #uuid "956627f2-fd1b-55e8-b3da-f529dc0dbe2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270581} {:answer/id #uuid "eafe7eb0-f4b1-5c2e-a83b-f7f47ae1704d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271328}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eafe7eb0-f4b1-5c2e-a83b-f7f47ae1704d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271328} :answer-field/id #uuid "31dc903f-41ad-5ad5-b457-03d1579fa938" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271600}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f63719f4-7a1e-5b43-8302-7786de4d0619" :question/math-academy-id "q-141199" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and $v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ is a solution of the equation $Ax=0?$\n\n- $\\text{Null}(A)={0}$\n- $A$ is not invertible\n- $\\text{Col}(A)\\ne {R}^{4}$" :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{4} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=4=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn. \n\n- Statement I is false. Since $v\\ne 0$ and $Av=0,$ \nthen $v\\in \\text{Null}(A)$ and, therefore, $\\text{Null}(A)\\ne {0}.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $\\text{Null}(A)\\ne {0},$ then according to the invertible matrix theorem, the matrix $A$ can't be invertible.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $A$ is not invertible, then by the invertible matrix theorem, we must have $\\text{Col}(A)\\ne {R}^{4}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272239 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124511 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "084a0a83-5ce6-5184-beb7-8ae7b4f1a799" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "188d8c1d-5896-5f10-81c9-bd96ad0517aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269475} {:answer/id #uuid "52e6f0c7-0a62-59e3-a5cb-80e9b7f54638" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270005} {:answer/id #uuid "54208619-14dc-516d-b1aa-20ed09a0bb47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270018} {:answer/id #uuid "77353fd7-ee88-5664-aec8-e00589b061d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270324} {:answer/id #uuid "af2612fc-39ba-5012-92ba-66032676ab46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270827}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "af2612fc-39ba-5012-92ba-66032676ab46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270827} :answer-field/id #uuid "94b73dce-7f8c-597e-ae8e-4e20e2b2ff1f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271774}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8dad0d98-37c5-5af9-9c0a-4f0fabec2bcc" :question/math-academy-id "q-141200" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $6\\times 6$ matrix and  $det(A)=-1?$ \n\nI. $A$ is invertible\n\nII. $\\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\}$\n\nIII. $\\text{nullity}(A)=6$" :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is a $6\\times 6$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{6} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=6=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn. \n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed,   a matrix $A$ is invertible if and only if $det(A)\\ne 0.$ Since here we have $det(A)=-1\\ne 0,$ our matrix $A$ must be  invertible.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, by the invertible matrix theorem\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,\\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $A$ is invertible,  it must be $\\text{nullity}(A)=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272287 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124521 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9d77214-796e-558f-a364-00ae3e25cc13" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30c30111-8967-5105-a0b6-6a23513210c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269710} {:answer/id #uuid "631335b6-7ef6-59ce-8e7e-749eea9ebc94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270145} {:answer/id #uuid "668c0601-7469-5a85-8564-3965eb0e1791" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270180} {:answer/id #uuid "bf4f4ba1-902f-5cdd-9b4c-bb432b2696b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270962} {:answer/id #uuid "dfabd0e7-90be-5aeb-9da3-657d3cdb0121" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271230}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bf4f4ba1-902f-5cdd-9b4c-bb432b2696b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270962} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d9f08da-5269-5b1f-bbcd-8c03985365d8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271593}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c894da3b-5f0a-59a9-8060-52795d04ecee" :question/math-academy-id "q-141460" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true given that $T:{R}^{7}\\to {R}^{7}$ is a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T,$ and $T(x)=v$ has no solution for some vector $v?$ \n\n- $T$ is not invertible\n- $\\text{nullity}(T)=0$\n- $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 7$" :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{7}$ into ${R}^{7},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{7} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=7 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $T(x)=v$ has no solution, we have that $v\\notin \\text{Im}(T),$ hence $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{7}.$ According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{7},$ the transformation $T$ and the matrix $T$ are not invertible.\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $T$ is not invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $T$ is not invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 7.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}]}
